What is the historical context of Lambert I, Count Of Louvain's life and reign?
Based on the provided context, Lambert I, Count of Louvain lived in the 10th and 11th centuries, specifically in the Duchy of Lotharingia, East Francia (now part of modern-day Belgium and the Netherlands). He was born around 950 and died on September 12, 1015. During his life, he was involved in a battle with Godfrey II, Duke of Lower Lorraine, and was killed in the process.

Lambert I was the first Count of Louvain, appointed in 1003, and was the son of Reginar III, Count of Hainaut, and Adela d'Eguisheim. He was married to Gerberga of Lower Lorraine and had a son, also named Lambert I.

The historical context of his life and reign is set against the backdrop of the Holy Roman Empire, with the Duchy of Lotharingia and the County of Verdun being significant territories in the region. The context also mentions the involvement of Holy Roman Emperor Henry III, who would later play a role in the life and reign of Lambert II, Count of Louvain, the son of Lambert I.
